;; The classes of bin/java-check's non-passing tests (doc/classes/JAVA-CONFORMANCE-SURVEY.md, §6.1,
;; "As built: JC1"): every test of test/java/check-LEVEL.edn that does not pass falls into the first
;; class whose :level is its level (or that has none) and one of whose :match regexps finds its
;; "RESULT REASON" (as bin/java-check writes them in .tmp/java-check/results.tsv); a test no class
;; takes fails the check (java.check/report). :kind says whose the work is: :world (a JDK class or
;; member jrt lacks, deferred, JCS9 (b): JC6 ranks them), :design (out of the Go build for good,
;; J2G-SPEC §12), :interp (the interpreter's bugs and limits: JC4), :analysis (the Java forms'
;; analysis refusing forms javac accepted: JC5, JCS11 (c)), :repl (the REPL's model: JC4, JCS8),
;; :repr-java (the converter: JCS12), :j2g (j2g's translation: the compiled level, JC2), :javac (javac
;; on Arbace's own Java, overlay/jdk/javac: its command line and file manager; the diagnostics level,
;; JC3), :speed, :harness (the check's runner, not Arbace), :other. A class nobody decided yet has
;; :decided :pending and names its :proposal.
;; Read with the Clojure reader.
[
 {:id :speed
  :title "speed: beyond the check's limit (20 s an action, the definition included)"
  :kind :speed
  :why "the interpreter is 5-200 times slower than HotSpot (CLASSFORMS-REPL §6); HotSpot's JIT tests loop 10^5-10^6 times to warm the JIT"
  :decided "survey §6.1, JCS10 (a)"
  :match [#"^timeout "]}
 {:id :read-failed
  :title "not interpreted or compiled: the read level failed (the test's class at that level)"
  :kind :harness
  :why "no forms to interpret or translate; the read level's result says why"
  :decided "As built: JC1"
  :match [#"^unread "]}
 ;; the tests whose result depends on timing or chance, by name (JCS71 (a)): bin/java-check runs a
 ;; would-be regression again once, and a test whose second run passes is reported flaky; it is no
 ;; regression when a class here names it, and in no class (the check failing) until one does. A
 ;; class of the level: a test flaky at one level is not at another (Test8267042's interp output
 ;; differs every run, LockStep's interp level fails to load ObjectInputStream every run)
 {:id :flaky
  :title "flaky: the result depends on the threads' timing or a Random's draw; passed when run again (JCS71 (a)), or fails by chance"
  :kind :harness
  :level :compiled
  :unstable true
  :why "jdk/java/lang/ref/EnqueuePollRace counts the references the collector has enqueued (99 of 100 once, on the loaded host; JC6); jdk/java/lang/RuntimeTests/exec/ExecWithInput prints lines of two processes' interleaving (4388 bytes against the JDK's 4580 once; passed when run again); compiler/c1/Test8267042 prints as two threads interleave (174 bytes against the JDK's 137 on the loaded host); jdk/java/util/List/LockStep serializes a list when its Random says so (maybe(4)), reaching ObjectOutputStream, cut (D6), by chance: both passed in JC2's compiled run and not in JC2b's; REAP9: EnqueuePollRace and LockStep failed on both runs of a would-be regression, so a test named here by :tests (the class :unstable) is no regression whatever its results (test/java/check.ae)"
  :decided "JCS71 (a) (doc/classes/JAVA-CONFORMANCE-SURVEY.md, \"Decided (2026-10-25): JCS70-JCS79\", \"As built: harness follow-ups\")"
  :tests ["t03289" "t04602" "t04819" "t05565"]}
 ;; the nread level (JFS123 (b), doc/classes/JAVA-FRONTEND-SURVEY.md, "As built: JN9f"): a test read without javac
 ;; whose files fell back to javac (the forms javac's: the reason the read without javac's), or whose forms differ
 ;; from javac's read; the classes are the next ports' list (JN9f's proposals)
 {:id :nread-constant
  :title "nread: a constant folded out of a long's range (a float or double constant cast to long, ConstFold)"
  :kind :repr-java
  :level :nread
  :why "the native provider's ConstFold converts a double constant to long by a checked conversion where Java's narrowing saturates (HotSpot's c1/c2 tests' (long) 1e38 constants)"
  :decided "JFS123 (b): the next ports (doc/classes/JAVA-FRONTEND-SURVEY.md, \"As built: JN9f\")"
  :proposal "JFS130"
  :match [#"^fallback .*Value out of range for long"]}
 {:id :nread-names
  :title "nread: a qualified member type or creation the declarations tier does not resolve (A.B.C, outer.new Inner(), a type imported on demand)"
  :kind :repr-java
  :level :nread
  :why "the declarations tier finds no type for a member class named through a qualifier the corpora never used (.Inner5.Inner6, X.Inner, BiFunction), and declines the qualified creation's qualifier"
  :decided "JFS123 (b): the next ports (doc/classes/JAVA-FRONTEND-SURVEY.md, \"As built: JN9f\")"
  :proposal "JFS130"
  :match [#"^fallback .*(no type |the qualified creation|a qualified creation)"]}
 {:id :nread-scope
  :title "nread: the scope tier declines (an assignment, a capture in an early construction context, a local class's number)"
  :kind :repr-java
  :level :nread
  :why "the scope tier's ports cover the corpora's code; these tests' flow (JDK 22's statements before super(), annotations' local classes) is not in them"
  :decided "JFS123 (b): the next ports (doc/classes/JAVA-FRONTEND-SURVEY.md, \"As built: JN9f\")"
  :proposal "JFS130"
  :match [#"^fallback .*(:scope/|a local class's number)"]}
 {:id :nread-inference
  :title "nread: the overload and inference tiers decline or fail (most specific of generic functional descriptors, merged descriptors, an argument context, an intersection target, a cycle, a choice absent or ambiguous)"
  :kind :repr-java
  :level :nread
  :why "langtools' lambda and generics tests exercise Resolve's and Infer's corners the JDK's own sources do not (MostSpecificNN, LambdaConvNN, T6531075, T5073060a)"
  :decided "JFS123 (b): the next ports (doc/classes/JAVA-FRONTEND-SURVEY.md, \"As built: JN9f\")"
  :proposal "JFS130"
  :match [#"^fallback .*:(overload|infer|types|flow)/"]}
 {:id :nread-differ
  :title "nread: the forms read without javac differ from javac's (none since JFS133 (a): a guard, a wrong native answer is a bug to fix, not a class to keep)"
  :kind :repr-java
  :level :nread
  :why "the native provider answers where javac answers otherwise, on code the corpora do not hold: nojavac-check's corpora read every file byte for byte, these tests were their first differences (JN9f: Numbers, TypeAnnotationReflection, T8008769, Pos03, LambdaCannotCapturePatternVariables, ExpressionSwitchBugsInGen); JFS133 (a) fixed all six (a double constant's remainder, a type annotation on the outermost enclosing type, a diamond's bound without its annotations, a qualified anonymous diamond's type, a conditional whose arm names its own binding, an intersection's Object methods)"
  :decided "JFS123 (b): the next ports, first; JFS133 (a) (doc/classes/JAVA-FRONTEND-SURVEY.md, \"As built: JFS133 (a), the six differences\")"
  :proposal "JFS133"
  :match [#"^differ the forms differ from the read level's"]}
 ;; the print level (REAP5, doc/FULL-SUPPORT-SURVEY.md, "As built: REAP5"; RPS9 (a)): a test's first
 ;; step not passing, of print, javac, forms, text, classes (java.print, test/java/print.ae); here,
 ;; before the classes of no level, whose regexps would take the printed Java's messages
 {:id :print-release
  :title "print: javac on Arbace refuses --release N (no ct.sym in its platform), as for the original (the classes level)"
  :kind :world
  :level :print
  :why "the test's @compile options hold --release 8 or 17; javac on Arbace attributes against the JDK build tree's exploded modules, which hold no ct.sym (JAVA-CONFORMANCE-SURVEY §5.5): the printed Java is refused as the original is"
  :decided "JCS50 (a), as :read-platform and :diag-release (doc/classes/JAVA-CONFORMANCE-SURVEY.md, \"Decided (2026-10-25): JCS50-JCS55\")"
  :match [#"release version \d+ not supported"]}
 {:id :print-type-arguments
  :title "print: javac rejects the printed Java for want of Java's type arguments in code (inference, captured and type-variable types, generic conversions)"
  :kind :repr-java
  :level :print
  :why "Java's type arguments in code are not in the forms (SPEC §1, principle 4), and where the original's typing needed them (a generic method's inference from wildcard-typed arguments, a captured type's cast, an explicit type argument, a lambda's parameter types through generic calls, a raw array of a generic type; RemoveMicroBenchmark's Stream.<Collection<Integer>>of(...), whose lambdas' parameters javac then types Object: cannot find symbol, REAP5b) the printer's casts do not recover them: the conformance corpus's kin of the 39 jdk files of bin/repr-java roundtrip (PRINTER-NOTES.md, \"javac rejects the printed Java\")"
  :decided "RPS9 (a): deferred until JFS75 (b)'s model carries type arguments (doc/FULL-SUPPORT-SURVEY.md, \"Decided (2026-10-24): RPS1-RPS17\")"
  :tests ["t05474"]
  :match [#"^fail javac rejects the printed Java: .*(inference variable|cannot infer type|cannot be applied to given types|no suitable (method|constructor)|CAP#|incomparable types|is ambiguous|bad return type in|is not a functional interface|incompatible types: invalid method reference|cannot be converted to [\w.$]+<|incompatible types: Object cannot be converted to|incompatible types: [A-Z]\d* cannot be converted|cannot be converted to [A-Z]\d*(\s|$))"]}
 {:id :print-shapes
  :title "print: the printed Java is read to another shape of the same Java (a when for an if, a do for an if in a loop's tail, a converter temporary's name, a label's or a constant's shape)"
  :kind :design
  :level :print
  :why "the converter reads one Java construct in more than one shape, by what surrounds it: an if as (when ...) or (if ... nil), a branch's tail with the recur inside a do or in each branch, a pattern test as when-instance or as (when (if-instance ...)), a loop's labels doubled, its temporaries numbered as it meets them and named o-30, a-31, old-1 (Java cannot spell a dash: the printed Java names them o_30, and the numbering moves with what is printed), a constant folded or not by the qualifier through which a static final is named (inner2.x against Inner2.x), a mutable local's tag; the printed Java has the same meaning and compiles, and the read gives the shape of its own text (the forms level of bin/repr-java roundtrip has the same kinds, PRINTER-NOTES.md, \"The round trip\")"
  :decided "RPS64 (a): restructurings by design, the temporaries' names with them (doc/FULL-SUPPORT-SURVEY.md, \"Decided (2026-10-25): RPS60-RPS78\", \"As built: v2 small leftovers B\")"
  :tests ["t03441" "t03447" "t03455" "t03456" "t03457" "t03659" "t03668" "t04680" "t05077" "t05214" "t05496" "t05864" "t05905"
          "t05935" "t06262" "t06349" "t06402" "t06403"]}
 {:id :print-erasure
  :title "print: the printed Java is read to forms with the casts and tags of Java's type arguments (an erasure cast, a for-each variable's type, a conditional's lub hint)"
  :kind :design
  :level :print
  :why "the forms carry no type arguments (SPEC §1, principle 4): the original's (.next i) of an Iterator<T> and its raw for-each variable are untyped, and the printer, which must write the type javac needs, prints (T) i.next() or for (T o : c), which the converter reads as the cast or the tag the original source wrote differently; the same meaning, the same class files but for the casts. Likewise a record pattern's ^:unconditional (JCS114 (a)): a switch's selector cast to a parameterized type is printed raw (switch ((Pair<String, Integer>) (Object) x) as switch ((Pair) (Object) x)), so javac infers no String for case Pair(String s, Integer i)'s component, the pattern is a test again, and the read gives a plain type pattern (TypedDeconstructionPatternExc, JN9f)"
  :decided "RPS9 (a), RPS64 (a): type arguments are not in the forms until JFS75 (b)'s model carries them (doc/FULL-SUPPORT-SURVEY.md, \"As built: v2 small leftovers B\")"
  :tests ["t03935" "t04728" "t05296" "t05351" "t05432" "t05472" "t05543" "t05676" "t06077" "t06080" "t06357" "t06376" "t06408" "t07835" "t07840" "t07844" "t08243"]}
 {:id :print-overload-cast
  :title "print: the forms lose a cast that chooses an overload (v((Object) \"s\") read as v(\"s\")): the printed Java calls another method"
  :kind :repr-java
  :level :print
  :why "the JLS corpus's Overloads: the converter drops the cast to Object of a String argument (it is a widening, javac's attribution keeps it only to choose v(Object)), so the forms, the printed Java and the interpreter (:interp-jls) call v(String); found by the print level's class files (invokestatic v:(Ljava/lang/Object;) against v:(Ljava/lang/String;)), the forms equal F1 (they lost it at the read)"
  :decided "RPS65 (a): deferred to JC5, the converter keeping a cast that changes javac's overload choice (doc/FULL-SUPPORT-SURVEY.md, \"Decided (2026-10-25): RPS60-RPS78\")"
  :proposal "RPS65"
  :tests ["t03216"]}
 {:id :print-classes
  :title "print: the printed Java's class files differ from the original's by what the forms do not record (a value's control flow, a local's initializer, fall-through, an increment's place, the loops' restructuring, the order of code or of the constant pool)"
  :kind :design
  :level :print
  :why "the forms record no source's choice of ?: against if or of a switch expression against a statement, Java's default value of a local written or not, a switch's fall-through (the following arms' code repeated), i++'s place, a loop's continue against the if's other arm: javac compiles the printed Java to other code of the same meaning, as for bin/repr-java roundtrip's classes level (PRINTER-NOTES.md, \"The class files\"; the forms level of this test passes: the printed Java holds the forms)"
  :decided "RPS9 (a): print's class files against the original's under bin/repr-java roundtrip's classes (doc/FULL-SUPPORT-SURVEY.md, \"Decided (2026-10-24): RPS1-RPS17\"); PRINTER-NOTES.md, \"The class files\" (the rest by design)"
  :match [#"^differ classes: "]}
